title grow the Rishi find primitive — extract, {n,m}, word boundary
prompt (Keaton) c2, grow find next
think find is the extract companion to matches; building it revealed its real consumer (claim_preserve) also needs counted repetition {n,m} and word boundary \b — its token patterns are \b(?:0x)?[0-9a-fA-F]{8,}\b — so the cohesive extract capability wants all three, and I added them together
think find returns every non-overlapping leftmost-longest match; implemented via search_anchored (longest match starting exactly at a position) looped left to right — a non-matching start fails as soon as its threads die, so find stays O(n·m) linear rather than quadratic
think {n,m} is expanded inline in the parser into required copies plus optional copies or a trailing star, so the NFA stays a plain program with no counting state; m is bounded so a huge count meets a clear error
think \b/\B are zero-width assertions checked against neighbouring bytes; threaded input through add_thread so the VM can see the boundary
think two-pass find (count, allocate exact, fill) so nothing is over-allocated; the matches boolean keeps its own single-pass path
obs validated against Python re — 69 module selftest cases, 396 matches cases with the new syntax vs re.search, 210 find cases vs re.finditer; all agree
obs the one documented semantic boundary — order-sensitive alternation — is leftmost-longest (POSIX, what an NFA gives): find returns abc for ab|abc where Python leftmost-first returns ab; every greedy seam pattern agrees, and for boolean matches it never shows
obs tame_style_check clean on main.rye and match.rye (only the pre-existing REDS 64 py fossil); rune and lower witnesses still GREEN
obs the regex gap is now closed for both halves; what remains before a seam is fully native is sort/unique (claim_preserve emits sorted-unique), loop accumulation, and user functions — sort/unique is the next small lap
